THE aarch64 INTER MEMORY REGRESSION IS TWO COMMITS, BOTH ISAs AGREE, AND THE
BIG ONE WAS THREE COPIES OF THE REFERENCE PICTURE. FIXED: 1.311x -> 1.189x AT
2048 AND 1.263x -> 1.042x AT 1280 ON aarch64; x86 RSS 1.185x -> 1.022x.

date:   2026-09-04
what:   the obligation `benchmarks/mem_inter_axis_2026-09-03b.meta` §2 left
        ("nobody has taken a clean per-commit binary pair across them, and
        this file does not"), then the fix the attribution supports.
data:   benchmarks/mem_refclone_2026-09-04.tsv (every number below).
tools:  rust/tools/mem_bisect.sh (NEW — round-robin peak RSS / peak heap over
        N perf_encode binaries on one cell, so box drift lands on every
        binary equally), rust/tools/mem_peak.sh, `vmmap -summary` and
        `heap -s` sampled every ~50 ms (macOS), heaptrack_print (Linux).
hosts:  mac (Apple M4 Pro, aarch64-apple-darwin, 16 KiB pages, libmalloc):
        `/usr/bin/time -l` max RSS, 7 rounds. r7900x (x86_64-linux):
        `/usr/bin/time -v` max RSS, 5 rounds, and heaptrack peak heap (LIVE
        bytes). Every mac number is RSS — heaptrack does not exist there.
trees:  seventeen binaries, one per commit of `940b855a..a1a32ca2f` (main's
        linear order), each built AT that sha on each host; every binary ran
        to completion with a non-empty .obu on every round (the harness
        excludes a refused encode and printed 0 refusals).
oracle: the same Bin/Release/libSvtAv1Enc.a as the two 2026-09-03 records;
        NOT rebuilt. Re-measured this session: mac 52992 / 119440 KiB
        (record: 53056 / 119488, -0.1 % / -0.04 %) — C did not move.

=================== 1. PER-COMMIT ATTRIBUTION — TWO STEPS, NOTHING ELSE =====

x86 peak HEAP over the seventeen commits (2048 inter / 1280 inter, MB):

  940b855a2 .. 64cb668ab   112.73 / 45.40    flat
  4e29d8fa7                121.56 / 49.18    +8.83 / +3.78   <- STEP 1
  ab7c5ed45 .. 96083a8e6   121.56 / 49.18    flat, identical to the digit
  8fa2d0353                123.14 / 49.80    +1.58 / +0.62   <- STEP 2
  eb658f4b7 .. a1a32ca2f   123.14 / 49.80    flat (0ba430ee1: 123.13)

x86 peak RSS says the same thing (2048: 115244 -> 124888 at 4e29d8fa7,
124840 -> 126812 at 8fa2d0353, every other adjacent pair within 0.3 %) and
so does the mac MIN, which is the readable statistic there because the
inter arm's run-to-run spread is 8-15 % and entirely UPWARD (thread-timing:
`SVTAV1_THREADS=1` at 1280 is 66864 [66832,66880] where auto is
[67072,90688]): 2048 min 150064 -> 161184 at 4e29d8fa7 (+10.9 MB); 1280 min
59632 -> 63952 at 4e29d8fa7 (+4.2 MB) and 63136 -> 67024 at 8fa2d0353
(+3.8 MB).

**The level scratch `700357e2b` is a NULL in this series on every quantity:**
x86 heap identical to the digit, x86 RSS +0.1 %, mac min 63856 -> 63936 at
1280 and 163056 -> 162800 at 2048. `benchmarks/mem_levelscratch_2026-09-03.meta`
§2 measured it as +4.1 MiB at 1280 against a base of 59.7 MiB; in this
series the +4 MiB at 1280 sits at `4e29d8fa7`, which precedes it on main,
and the binary AT `ab7c5ed45` (that record's base sha) already reads
63856 min. The two measurements were not taken from the same tree lineage
(that record built its pair on the level-scratch branch while the inter
commits were landing) and this file does not reconcile them further; what
it establishes is that on main's history the level scratch carries no
measurable share of the regression on either ISA.

`mem_inter_axis_2026-09-03b.meta` §2's hypothesis — "four inter correctness
fixes give the port reference state it did not previously hold" — is half
right. STEP 2 (`8fa2d0353`, the temporal motion field) is exactly that: a
per-reference `Vec<MvRef>` (65,536 cells at 2048) that C also stores. STEP 1
is not.

=================== 2. STEP 1 WAS THREE COPIES OF ONE PICTURE ===============

heaptrack's peak-instant contributors on the tip (2048 inter, x86; the
instant is frame 1's DPB refresh, because both frames' DPB entries AND the
caller's un-stored `ReferenceFrame` are live together):

  27.69 M  DecodedPictureBuffer::refresh   — `Arc::new(frame.clone())` of a
                                             `&ReferenceFrame` whose owner is
                                             still alive: BOTH frames' entries
                                             (2 x ~13.9 MB)
   7.15 M  PaddedPlane::from_plane          — the caller's padded planes, the
                                             ORIGINAL the clone above copied
   7.15 M  encode_frame_impl (closure)      — `rf.padded.clone()` per inter
                                             frame (pipeline.rs:2719)
   6.29 M  Vec<u8>::clone x3                — recon y/u/v into the caller's
                                             `ReferenceFrame`
   4.19 M  Vec::from_elem                   — `rf.y_plane.clone()` per inter
                                             frame (pipeline.rs:2712)
  17.18 M  PaPlane::refill_from_plane       — the PA pyramids (C has these)
  16.80 M  partition::funnel_block_decision — 4,096 x (32x32 i32 qcoeffs):
                                             per-block retention until the
                                             pack; C keeps 24 KiB/SB of the
                                             same (`quantized_coeff`)
  10.49 M  tx_pipeline::tx_unit_inner       — the winners' qcoeff/recon,
                                             same retention
   6.29 M  perf_encode (x2)                 — the harness's frames

So at the peak instant the LAST reference existed THREE times: in the DPB
slot, as `ref_frame_data` (a `Vec<u8>` clone of `y_plane`), and as
`ref_padded_luma` (a `Box<PaddedRef>` clone) — and the picture being STORED
existed twice, as the caller's value and as `refresh`'s clone of it.
`4e29d8fa7` introduced the two per-frame clones when it made the DPB
receive inter frames (until then `self.dpb.get(slot)` was `None` on every
frame and both clones were of nothing), and their sizes ARE its step:
y_plane 4.19 M + padded (y 4.77 + u/v 2.38) 7.15 M = 11.3 M allocated,
8.83 M at the peak instant.

=================== 3. THE FIX, AND WHAT IT MEASURES =========================

Two changes, both lifetime/size and neither churn (the rules
`benchmarks/mem_heaptrack_satd_2026-09-03.meta` and
`neighbor_scratch_ab_2026-09-03.meta` paid for):

  * `DecodedPictureBuffer::refresh` takes the `ReferenceFrame` BY VALUE. The
    caller's value was dead after the call; the `frame.clone()` was a
    transient duplicate of the whole picture alive at the peak instant.
  * The frame holds the LAST slot's own `Arc<ReferenceFrame>`
    (`DecodedPictureBuffer::get_shared`, new) and derives `&[u8]` /
    `&PaddedRef` from it, instead of deep-cloning `y_plane` and `padded`.
    The slot is read-only once stored and the DPB is not refreshed until the
    end of the frame, so it reads the bytes the clone read.

Interleaved A/B, tip vs candidate, same session (medians, min in brackets):

  cell           host    tip                    cand1                 delta
  2048 inter     mac    156624 [156528]        142032 [142000]       -14.6 MB
  1280 inter     mac     66944 [66864]          55216 [54400]        -11.7 MB
  2048 inter     x86    126596 [126392]        109220 [109192]       -17.4 MB
  1280 inter     x86     54348 [53944]          45328 [44764]         -9.0 MB
  2048 heap      x86    123.13 M               101.78 M              -21.35 M
  1280 heap      x86     49.80 M                40.90 M               -8.90 M

`obu_bytes` identical on every row (139761 / 81815), and the mac threads=1
form agrees (2048 min 156640 -> 139952; 1280 66832 -> 55136).

**Ratios against C, now:** mac 2048 inter **1.189x** (was 1.311x), 1280
**1.042x** (was 1.263x); x86 RSS 1.022x / 0.920x (was 1.185x / 1.104x);
x86 peak heap 0.847x / 0.799x. Both aarch64 inter cells are back INSIDE
the 25 % goal, and further inside than the 2026-09-03 "after" column
(1.257x / 1.121x) they regressed from.

One correction to the record this chunk was briefed on: "x86 RSS 1.086x
(passing)" was the 2026-09-03 tree. The tip before this fix measured
**1.185x** on x86 too (126596 vs 106868) — the regression was not
aarch64-only; the aarch64 EXCESS over x86 is what is.

=================== 4. THE aarch64 EXCESS THAT REMAINS, DECOMPOSED ===========

`vmmap -summary` at the peak sample, mac, threads=1, 2048 inter, resident
MiB (full rows in the .tsv §6):

                     MALLOC_LARGE  LARGE(empty)  MALLOC_SMALL  SMALL(empty)
  C                      98.1          —             14.0          —
  port, tip              20.6         39.7           55.9         35.8
  port, cand1            20.6         31.1           57.0         28.6

"(empty)" is libmalloc's own label: regions holding NO live block that it
still keeps resident — freed large blocks parked in the large cache, and
fully-free 4 MiB small regions parked in the depot — with their pages marked
reusable but still counted by `/usr/bin/time -l` (the SMALL rows' dirty
column is well below their resident column for the same reason). C's malloc
zones have no empty regions at its peak. That is the resident-not-live
mechanism `mem_aarch64_2026-09-03.meta` §2c inferred: it is not retention
POLICY (§2b's knobs), it is that libmalloc keeps freed regions resident
until memory pressure and glibc does not. The fix removed 8.6 MiB of it
(freed padded/plane clones no longer exist to be parked) and 7.2 MiB of
empty small regions; ~60 MiB of empties remain at the peak sample, which is
the residual gap between the two ISAs (mac 142032 vs x86 109220 KiB).

`heap -s` over the same encode puts the port's LIVE small/tiny-zone bytes at
<= ~45 MB at any sample (x86 heaptrack agrees: 44.5 MB of the peak is the
three per-block sites above) against ~85 MiB resident in the small zone —
the 2x of §2c — and those blocks are the per-block coefficient/recon
retention C also has. Shrinking them is a per-block SIZE change with a
byte-identity surface, not something this record's attribution supports;
it is the next thing to measure if the aarch64 margin is ever needed.

Not done, and why: STEP 2 (`8fa2d0353`, +1.58 M at 2048) is the saved
motion field — the per-reference `mvs` (`(mi_rows+1)/2 x (mi_cols+1)/2` =
65,536 cells of a 6-byte `MvRef` at 2048, 0.39 M per stored frame) plus
the projection's `tpl_mvs` — at the SAME 8x8 density C stores its 8-byte
`MV_REF` (`coding_unit.h:44`). It is state C also holds, sized no larger
than C's, and it is 1 % of the gap this chunk closed.

=================== 3b. THE STILL AND VIDEOKEY ARMS MOVE TOO — MEASURED, NOT
                        PREDICTED ==============================================

The first draft of this record said the user-priority arms could not move
because the key frame takes neither per-frame clone. That was wrong about
`refresh`: a key frame is stored with `refresh_frame_flags = 0xFF`, so its
`ReferenceFrame` was cloned in full — planes AND padded planes — while the
original was alive, on the STILL path as much as on the inter one. The
canonical `tools/mem_peak.sh` run on the candidate tree (mac, both sides,
median of 5, .tsv §7):

  cell            before   cand1    C        before/C  cand1/C
  1280 still       35792    28256    45376     0.788     0.623
  1280 videokey    41984    33664    47552     0.883     0.708
  2048 still       81520    72624    95808     0.851     0.758
  2048 videokey    96192    87856   111536     0.901     0.788
  2048 inter      158864   142640   119568     1.329     1.193

`obu_bytes` identical to C on every still and videokey row (34293 / 81771 /
70893 / 139700). -7.5 to -8.9 MB on the still arm at every size, i.e. one
padded-plus-unpadded copy of the picture, which is what the transient was.

